In the above

\[-2\pi<\arg\;\sqrt{q}\;\cosh z<\pi\] \[|\cosh z-\sigma|>|\sigma\pm 1|,\;\mathscr{R}>0,\]

but \(\sigma\) is otherwise arbitrary. If \(\sigma^{2}=1\), **20.9.2** and **20.9.4** become three-term recurrence relations.

Formulas **20.9.1** and **20.9.3** are valid for arbitrary \(a\), \(q\), provided \(r\) is also known; they give multiples of **20.4.12**, normalized so as to approach the corresponding Hankel functions \(H_{1}^{(n)}(\sqrt{q}e^{s})\), \(H_{2}^{(n)}(\sqrt{q}e^{s})\), as \(z\to\infty\). See [20.36], section 2.63. The formula is especially useful if \(|\cosh z|\)is large and \(q\) is not too large; thus if \(\sigma=-1\), the absolute ratio of two successive terms in the expansion is essentially

\[\left|\left(\frac{\sqrt{q}}{m}+\frac{m}{4\sqrt{q}}+2\right)\left(\cosh\;z+1 \right)\right|.\]

If \(a\), \(q\), \(z\), \(v\) are real, the real and imaginary components of \(Mc_{r}^{(n)}(z,\,q)\) are \(Mc_{r}^{(1)}(z,\,q)\) and \(Mc_{r}^{(n)}(z,\,q)\), respectively; similarly for the components of \(M_{r}^{(n)}(z,\,q)\). If the parameters are complex

**20.9.5**: \(Mc_{r}^{(n)}(z,\,q)=\frac{1}{2}\left[Mc_{r}^{(n)}(z,\,q)+Mc_{r}^{(n)}(z,\,q)\right]\)
**20.9.6**: \(Mc_{r}^{(n)}(z,\,q)=-\frac{i}{2}\left[Mc_{r}^{(n)}(z,\,q)-Mc_{r}^{(n)}(z,\,q)\right]\)

Replacing \(e\) by \(s\) in the above will yield corresponding relations among \(M_{s}^{(n)}(z,q)\).

